@@ -98,7 +98,9 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields; deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) and HTTP/Livewire panels remain out of scope per #14.
+**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields; HTTP/Livewire panels remain out of scope per #14.
+
+**Call the next ticket:** domain action `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` is the attendant panel behind the three buttons (`App\Enums\CallMode`: `any`, `priority_only`, `normal_only`; no authorization — the caller is trusted). A ticket is **normal** when `priority = 0` and **priority** when `priority > 0`. Only `waiting` tickets of the given queue are candidates (other queues never selected; called tickets never recalled). Ordering is deterministic: higher `priority` first, then oldest `issued_at`, then lowest `id`. `priority_only` selects only priority tickets in that ordering, `normal_only` only normal tickets oldest first — both ignore fairness. In `any` mode the fairness rule applies: when the last 3 called tickets of the queue (most recent first by `called_at`, then by `id`) are all priority tickets and at least one normal ticket is waiting, the call is restricted to the oldest waiting normal (calls from every mode count in that history; a normal call resets the streak; with no normal waiting, priority tickets keep being called). The action runs in a transaction: it locks the queue row and re-reads `is_active` from the database (stale instances rejected with `App\Exceptions\InactiveQueueException`, changing nothing), picks the ordered candidate, then locks the candidate row and re-checks it is still `waiting` (skipping losers of a concurrent `Ticket::call()`), marks the winner `called` with `called_at = now()` and `called_by_user_id`, and returns the updated model (only `status`/`called_at`/`called_by_user_id` change, per the write-once rule). When no ticket is eligible for the requested mode, it throws `App\Exceptions\NoWaitingTicketsException`.
 
